WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.700
A mouse olfactory neuron carries about a thousand odorant receptor genes.

2
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.340
Yet a mature one transcribes just one gene, from just one copy.

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.840
That is called singular choice.

4
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.300
How does a cell make a choice that complete, and then keep it?

5
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:23.533
Start with silence.

6
00:00:24.453 --> 00:00:30.493
Picture a library where nearly every book is chained shut, and one is left open.

7
00:00:31.593 --> 00:00:36.373
In mouse olfactory neurons, receptor genes carry repressive marks.

8
00:00:37.173 --> 00:00:42.033
These are marks of heterochromatin, which is tightly packed, silenced chromatin.

9
00:00:42.973 --> 00:00:48.833
The data suggest this silencing comes before any receptor expression can be detected.

10
00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.867
Now read one result carefully.

11
00:00:52.807 --> 00:00:59.567
In mouse neurons labelled for a receptor called P2, the active copy carried less of

12
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.547
the marks than its inactive partner.

13
00:01:01.887 --> 00:01:04.787
So the chosen copy is less repressed.

14
00:01:05.547 --> 00:01:06.967
That is only a comparison.

15
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.567
It does not show that the chosen gene silenced the others.

16
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.747
The silencing was already there.

17
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.700
How does one gene escape?

18
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:22.280
An enhancer is a DNA element that raises the transcription of a gene it contacts.

19
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.680
In mouse neurons, receptor gene clusters on 18 chromosomes contact one another, in

20
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.860
hubs. Single-cell data indicate several competing hubs in developing neurons,

21
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.900
before one gene dominates.

22
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.260
The evidence supported a further step.

23
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.240
Receptor RNA suppresses the competing receptors at a distance.

24
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.420
The machinery for that is still unidentified.

25
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.510
Next, the neuron must keep its choice.

26
00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.650
Receptor expression engages the unfolded protein response, the pathway by which a

27
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.630
cell reacts to misfolded protein.

28
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.830
In developing mouse neurons, a kinase called PERK acts first.

29
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:12.870
Then a factor called ATF5 promotes the gene for adenylyl cyclase 3.

30
00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.050
That enzyme in turn relieves the stress.

31
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.910
In young mice lacking it, lineage tracing showed more switching.

32
00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.770
So the feedback helps stabilize the choice.

33
00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:28.203
Put it together. First comes silence.

34
00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:32.583
Then several hubs compete, and one gene dominates.

35
00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:36.383
Then feedback helps hold that choice in place.

36
00:02:37.683 --> 00:02:40.443
This mouse model is ordered but incomplete.

37
00:02:41.623 --> 00:02:44.543
Singular expression is the endpoint of a sequence.

38
00:02:45.103 --> 00:02:47.203
It is not a property of every stage.
